ent variables. If this assumption is violated (heteroscedasticity), it can affect the efficiency of the estimates.\n\n4. **Normality**: The residuals (errors) of the model should be normally distributed, particularly for small sample sizes. This assumption is important for hypothesis testing.\n\n5. **No multicollinearity**: Independent variables should not be too highly correlated with each other. High multicollinearity can inflate the variance of the coefficient estimates and make them unstable.\n\n**Importance**  \nUnderstanding Child Mortality through Multiple Regression Analysis is critical for public health research. By identifying the significant predictors of child mortality, such as socioeconomic status, access to healthcare, nutrition, and education, stakeholders can develop targeted strategies to reduce child deaths. This analysis not only highlights the direct effects of these factors but also allows for the exploration of interactions between them.\n\n**Applications**  \nMultiple Regression Analysis is widely applied in health economics, epidemiology, and social sciences to inform policy decisions. In the case of Child Mortality, it can be used to evaluate the effectiveness of health interventions, assess the impact of economic policies, and guide resource allocation in healthcare systems.\n\nIn summary, Multiple Regression Analysis serves as a powerful tool for analyzing the determinants of Child Mortality. By adhering to its underlying assumptions and accurately interpreting the results, researchers can provide valuable insights that contribute to improving child health outcomes and reducing mortality rates.", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": [], "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null}, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "protected_material_code": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "protected_material_text": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtere